Test execution runtime optimizations
===================================

Status
  Phase 1 is complete and accepted by the user on 2026-11-03, including the measured timing
  limitations. All later phases are pending. This plan covers the five prioritized optimizations
  from the test-runtime review and a separate water-test necessity review requested at acceptance.
  Creating the plan does not approve reductions in test coverage.

Purpose and scope
  Reduce unnecessary rendering, numerical setup and synchronization in VGE tests while
  preserving their independent numerical, lifecycle and state-restoration guarantees.
  Use existing production shader abstractions, fixture ownership and cached programs.
  Keep production rendering behavior unchanged. Scope is the VGE test project and narrowly
  justified test seams; discuss any required production behavior change before implementation.

Evidence and references
  The preceding review used TestResults/GpuTests11/final-qualification/final-debug.trx and
  final-release.trx. Recorded case-time sums were water receiver diagnostics 170s/3s,
  two atmosphere quality-policy cases 63s/32s, water continuity and budget suites 144s/14s,
  four surface-lighting families 65s/47s, and real-GC stress 23s/42s, Debug/Release respectively.
  These historical measurements include case setup and teardown. Concurrent case durations
  are not additive wall-clock savings. Confirm current source and receipt availability before
  using them as implementation baselines. The Debug/Release water disparity is unattributed.
  Earlier investigations provide context, with different workloads and source revisions.
  - [GPU.IntegrationTestPerformance.Analysis.md](GPU.IntegrationTestPerformance.Analysis.md)
  - [GPU.IntegrationTestPerformance.RemainingCosts.md](GPU.IntegrationTestPerformance.RemainingCosts.md)

Shared implementation and acceptance requirements
  Preserve scenarios, parameter rows, nominated coordinates, motion samples, independent
  reference calculations, tolerances, lifecycle assertions and failure diagnostics.
  Any proposed coverage change requires a concrete equivalence argument and user agreement.
  Keep resource ownership scoped to the existing fixture/context lifetime and initialize
  mutable inputs and result storage explicitly for each operation.
  Use configured test-writing/review agents and delegate every build/test invocation.
  Complete each coherent implementation batch before focused verification and independent
  review. Reuse valid evidence and avoid repeated full-suite runs between individual edits.
  Measure affected selections before and after under matched build, shader, fixture and cache
  conditions where practical. Record selection, counts, failures/skips, wall time and conditions.
  Separate build time, summed case duration, CPU synchronization time and GPU execution time.
  Use bounded attribution to resolve specific uncertainties. Do not infer causes or percentage
  improvements from unmatched Debug/Release or historical runs.
  No fixed performance threshold applies. Discuss the resulting metrics and limitations with
  the user and obtain acceptance before closing each phase.
  Each phase is a separate reviewable commit boundary. Staging and committing require a request.

☑ Phase 1 — Restrict water receiver diagnostics to observed pixels
  Implementation and measurement record (2026-11-03)
    The test-only fullscreen helper accepts a pipeline scissor while retaining full-target
    clears and the 128×128 viewport. Diagnostics draw one pixel, or a 64×1 strip containing
    every edge-motion observation. The shader and production rendering code are unchanged.
    Source review found no derivatives or implicit texture sampling in the reachable liquid
    kernels. All original rows, assertions and readbacks remain, with added viewport/scissor
    restoration assertions. The second review and independent completion audit found no
    required code corrections or verification gaps. The user accepted the result on 2026-11-03
    and requested the separate water-test necessity review below.
    Two baseline and two candidate runs per configuration each passed 72/72, zero failures or
    skips; all 102 logged pixel observations per candidate run matched baseline exactly.
    All 909 emitted test SPIR-V hashes per configuration matched baseline.
    Command wall seconds, excluding builds:
      Debug baseline 149.674 / 131.478; candidate 206.517 / 169.493.
      Release baseline 148.176 / 149.566; candidate 171.804 / 161.667.
    No measured wall-time improvement is established; every candidate run was slower than its
    configuration's baseline runs. Causes are unattributed. The historical Debug/Release
    disparity was not reproduced. These sequential fresh-process runs retained existing driver,
    filesystem and executable caches; no isolated synchronization or GPU execution times were
    collected. Builds used current -O shaders, with -g additionally in Debug.
    Receipts: ../TestResults/TestRuntimeOptimizations/water-receiver/summary.json, commands.txt,
    the eight TRX files, candidate-*-equivalence.json, candidate-*-shader-diff.json and
    candidate-source-stability.json. Build timings and the initial package-cache failure are
    retained separately there. Acceptance includes the absence of demonstrated wall-time savings
    and the unresolved cause of the earlier sequential-run slowdown.
  Slower-timing investigation (2026-11-03)
    The eight original receipts show a broad per-case shift, rather than a single outlier.
    A six-row selection (flat, edge-motion and positive-view-z; budget 8; both resolutions)
    compared full/scissored draws in ABBA and BAAB order with the same prepared inputs/program.
    Both Debug orders and both Release orders passed every original assertion and exact
    seven-attachment comparisons at the nominated pixels. First submissions took seconds in
    either mode; later RHI DrawIndexed scopes were sub-millisecond. Corrected Debug state-query/assert
    totals across four draws were 0.032–0.120 ms outside the first process case, whose totals
    were 2.849–3.236 ms. These checks cannot explain the earlier roughly 0.4-second case shift.
    Debug localized multi-second waits to the first RHI DrawIndexed call. The native-intercepted
    Release runs instead placed them in an unattributed render-boundary remainder; combining
    those two instrumentation layers into one driver-cause claim would be invalid. Warm GPU
    clear/pass/draw intervals were about 66–219 microseconds full versus 12–41 scissored;
    those elapsed query intervals can include idle time and are not exclusive shader timings.
    No consistent scissor-first penalty was reproduced. Variable first-use work dominates
    these samples, but the exact cause of the earlier sequential-run regression remains
    unresolved. Instrumented process wall times are attribution receipts, not matched speedups.
    Evidence is retained alongside the original receipts as investigation-*-scopes.txt,
    the corresponding TRX files and instrumented source snapshots. The superseded first
    profile's combined state/draw timing and the incomplete helper build are excluded evidence.
    Temporary instrumentation and comparison controls were removed; both live source files
    match their pre-investigation SHA-256 hashes exactly. Restored Debug/Release builds passed,
    all 909 shader hashes per configuration remained unchanged, and final focused checks passed
    6/6 each with unchanged logged values (27.852 / 25.032 seconds wall, builds excluded).
    See ../TestResults/TestRuntimeOptimizations/water-receiver/investigation-summary.json.
  Unchanged-candidate timing rerun (2026-11-03)
    The original 72-case selection passed twice per configuration, zero failures/skips.
    Command wall seconds: Debug 156.772 / 159.145; Release 159.949 / 167.525.
    All 102 logged pixel observations matched prior receipts exactly. The two source files,
    both test binaries and all 909 shader binaries per configuration remained unchanged;
    no rebuild was needed. Timings varied from the prior candidate runs, but every rerun
    remained slower than its configuration's original baseline. Cross-time differences do
    not establish a causal speedup. Receipt:
    ../TestResults/TestRuntimeOptimizations/water-receiver/rerun-now-20261103-033200-summary.json.
  ☑ Inspect WaterRefractionDiagnosticTests and water_refraction_diagnostics.fsh to map the
    one-pixel and six-pixel observations to their shader coordinate and derivative requirements.
  ☑ Choose a bounded raster approach, such as scissoring, that retains the 128×128 viewport,
    source textures and original fragment coordinates. Verify derivative/helper-invocation
    behavior and any attachment initialization requirements before selecting the approach.
  ☑ Eliminate unnecessary fragment execution across the seven diagnostic outputs while
    preserving every receiver scenario, budget, full/half-resolution variant and sampled value.
    Restore modified raster state through existing production test abstractions.
  ☑ Compare focused correctness and runtime against the original execution. Investigate the
    large Debug/Release disparity only as needed to attribute the affected work accurately.
  ☑ Complete second review, independent coverage audit and linked-document reconciliation.
    Present measured results and limitations for user acceptance before closing the phase.

☐ Phase 2 — Reevaluate water-test necessity and overlapping coverage
  ☐ Inventory the water receiver diagnostics, continuity and budget tests covered by this plan,
    including every scenario, parameter row, sampled field and independent reference assertion.
    Identify the production contract and failure mode each observation is intended to protect.
  ☐ Compare those guarantees with other existing water tests and current production behavior.
    Distinguish unique regression coverage, deliberate integration/lifecycle witnesses, redundant
    observations, obsolete behavior and checks that assert implementation details without a
    required contract. Similar inputs or outputs alone do not establish redundant coverage.
  ☐ For each proposed consolidation or removal, identify the exact tests/rows affected, the
    surviving independent witness for every required guarantee, and any coverage that would be
    lost. Explain why the remaining coverage would still detect the relevant failures; timing
    cost alone does not establish that a test is unnecessary. Retain unresolved cases.
  ☐ Present evidence-backed keep/consolidate/remove recommendations and their measured costs
    where available. Obtain the user's decision on proposed coverage changes before editing
    tests; this phase initially evaluates necessity and does not presume that any test is unneeded.
  ☐ Independently review the coverage mapping and recommendations against their source contracts.
    Record the user's dispositions and reconcile the later water-batching tasks with the agreed
    retained coverage and verification obligations. Scope any approved test changes explicitly;
    adding this phase does not authorize deleting tests or weakening assertions.

☐ Phase 3 — Separate atmosphere policy checks from repeated numerical construction
  ☐ Map the guarantees of QualityOnlyEditRebuildsLighting and
    AsyncCompletionAdmitsQualityOnlyChange in AtmosphereScatteringQualityTests, including
    replacement identity, revision, admission and completion behavior.
  ☐ Reuse the controllable-producer approach from AtmosphereComputationTests for policy-only
    checks. Retain an explicit real quality-change integration witness and all independent
    quadrature/numerical coverage. Discuss the exact separation before changing test boundaries.
  ☐ Identify identical default-table construction in SourceRemainsFiniteAndNightIsDark and
    DefaultQuadratureAgreesWithRefinement in AtmosphereMultipleScatteringTests. Reuse immutable
    tables keyed by all determining inputs where safe, preserving refined integrations and
    every aerosol/quality comparison. Avoid order dependence and unbounded retained storage.
  ☐ Verify policy failure detection, integrated replacement behavior, numerical equivalence
    and fixture isolation. Measure numerical builds avoided and affected-selection runtime.
  ☐ Complete second review, independent coverage audit and linked-document reconciliation.
    Present measured results and limitations for user acceptance before closing the phase.

☐ Phase 4 — Batch independent water motion observations
  Follow the water-test necessity decisions from Phase 2. Until a coverage change is explicitly
  agreed, the original sample counts and guarantees below remain the implementation contract.
  ☐ Map all 201-position shallow-water sweeps and 61-position budget sweeps to their inputs,
    outputs and CPU reference assertions. Identify any ordering or shared-state dependencies.
  ☐ Discuss and agree on a concrete indexed-result design before implementation. Batching was
    previously deferred; this phase supplies the specific design and coverage discussion.
  ☐ Render independent samples into indexed records and retrieve records together, preserving
    every original sample, original coordinate semantics and adjacent-sample comparison.
    Keep independent CPU geometric formulas and continuity thresholds unchanged.
  ☐ Retain precise failure attribution to scenario, motion position and output field, including
    the existing worst-jump diagnostics. Bound result storage and handle partial final batches.
  ☐ Verify equivalence to original observations and measure draw/dispatch counts, readback count,
    transferred bytes and affected-selection wall time. Account for added batching setup work.
  ☐ Complete second review, independent coverage audit and linked-document reconciliation.
    Present measured results and limitations for user acceptance before closing the phase.

☐ Phase 5 — Combine surface-lighting per-frame statistics
  ☐ Map Frame, Energy and RunUntil in SurfaceLightingConsumerRuntimeFixture and identify every
    caller relying on per-frame finiteness, energy, readiness or convergence observations.
  ☐ Design a compact combined statistics result that preserves each existing observed quantity
    and its numerical semantics while reducing repeated dispatch/readback pairs.
  ☐ Integrate the result through existing observation services and fixture-owned shader assets.
    Preserve per-frame failure detection, optional world-atlas handling and progress conditions.
    Moving assertions to final milestones requires separate coverage discussion and agreement.
  ☐ Verify failure detection and numerical bounds across the affected consumer, spatial,
    scenario and lifetime suites. Measure per-frame observation work and selection runtime.
  ☐ Complete second review, independent coverage audit and linked-document reconciliation.
    Present measured results and limitations for user acceptance before closing the phase.

☐ Phase 6 — Separate real-GC stress execution from frequent correctness feedback
  ☐ Inspect Workload=Stress classification, AllTests.runsettings and current local/CI entry
    points. Propose a frequent-correctness selection and a dedicated mandatory stress selection.
    Agree on invocation and CI cadence before changing the default selection.
  ☐ Keep GpuResourceAssociationStressTests intact, including its 12×32 cycles, abandoned aliases,
    forced collections, finalizer waits and retirement/pruning assertions.
  ☐ Make both selections explicit in runner configuration and usage documentation. Ensure the
    dedicated stress selection remains discoverable and required by the agreed qualification
    workflow, with no accidental omission or duplication in the combined coverage accounting.
  ☐ Validate selection membership and execute the affected stress coverage. Report faster
    frequent feedback separately from total qualification cost; moving work is not eliminating it.
  ☐ Reconcile all six phases' retained evidence and run one appropriately scoped combined
    qualification after the implementation batches are complete. Preserve failures and focused
    correction receipts and distinguish reconciled coverage from a fresh passing full run.
  ☐ Remove temporary instrumentation, comparison controls and temporary support documents
    created for this plan after retaining required decisions and evidence. Keep this tasklist,
    authoritative documentation and the linked historical performance analyses.
  ☐ Complete second review, independent coverage audit and documentation reconciliation.
    Discuss final metrics and remaining costs with the user and obtain acceptance before closure.
